title The bill a parked lap leaves
prompt the baton -- COPAL, lane amphora and bron-resins, next agent-doable lap
rota lap 4205, row 0 -- Aether, the row that hears: why the work exists. Read the threshold, then the Lindy page. It named this lap's finding before the lap did -- witness before narrative, because a checked claim stays checkable while an asserted one fades into the silence the row is trained to notice. A population typed into a guard's header is an asserted claim; the same population counted on every run is a checked one.
think Opened on a clean tree with ten stashes, the newest written at 20260907.191957 -- eleven seconds before my own clock read. That is the round open parking a lap, and the parked lap was mine.
think Read both new stashes before touching anything. Same six paths in each, an elder and a later draft of one round: the prose_register census, its control, its witness, one session log, one accounts shelf, one REDS fold shelf. The work had been written twice and sent zero times.
think git diff stash@{0}^ HEAD over the three tool files came back empty, so no peer had touched them in either park. That is what made the recovery mechanical rather than a merge.
think Recovered with git checkout stash@{0} -- <path>, which reads the tree object rather than the blob, so mode 100755 rode back with both fixtures. git show would have landed them 100644 and reddened exec_bit on the next lap.
think Ran the witness before writing a word of prose. GREEN, twenty assertions, the three census legs among them.
obs THE WORK RECOVERS WHOLE AND THE LEDGER DOES NOT. Every byte of the three tool files came back untouched; every ledger byte around them had moved. The row renumbered a third time -- 576 at the first park, 584 off the fetched spine now -- and both pins had returned to their ceilings, REDS at 38,636 of 40,960 and the card at 40,957 of 40,960, three bytes free.
obs So the bill of a park is paid in pins and numbers rather than in work. %578 folded to its own shelf to seat the new row, and my card block was rewritten six times against living_pin_max_bytes before it fit -- 1,900 bytes of account condensed to 890, with the detail folded onto the accounts shelf where a link can still reach it.
obs THE DRAFT LOG CARRIED A CLAIM THAT HAD GONE FALSE WHILE IT SAT. Two lines named %576 as the row folded for room; a peer folded %576 at 20260907.172225 while this lap was parked, so the draft would have shipped a path to a file it did not create. Corrected in the draft rather than errata'd after, because a stash is not testimony -- nothing had published, and correcting an unshipped draft costs one line where correcting a shipped one costs a row.
obs What the aether row heard: the finding and the fault are the same shape one level apart. The scan's header asserted a population; the draft log asserted a fold. Both were true when typed and neither was checked at the moment it shipped.
obs THE FIRST HOT PASS REFUSED BEFORE IT RAN A GUARD, AND THE ORPHAN WAS MINE. run_verdict=run_in_flight named pid 2654575 holding construction/standing-equipment-run.lock.d, started 19:18:56 -- the parked lap's own runner, detached, its group leader gone and its output reaching nobody. /proc/2654575/cwd resolved to this tree before I signalled it, and kill -TERM on that one pid ran the runner's own EXIT trap and released the lock, where pkill -f would have reached across a pier eight ships share (%569).
obs TWO REDS IN THE FIRST FULL PASS, NEITHER AUTHORED HERE, BOTH WITH A NAMED REPAIR. index_row_bound refused rows_misordered=1 on a peer's pair, 20260907.155612 standing above 20260907.165730, closed by the repair the scan itself advises. plant refused sourcing=14 against a floor spelling 13: a fourteenth control adopted the plant law and the floor did not follow, which is the assert's own instruction -- raise this line when a lane adopts. Raised, with the say line beside it. standing_equipment was the third and it was the mirror of the other two.
obs THE REBASE BROUGHT TWO MORE REDS AND NEITHER WAS AUTHORED HERE EITHER. Merging the day shelf misordered it a second time -- 20260907.182550 above 20260907.192817 -- closed again by index_shelf_repair.sh, which is %440's class firing on this seat within one round. fold_shelf_link then refused fold_depth_lost=5 on a peer's new accounts shelf: four links written at the card's depth, archive/NAME where a file already standing inside archive/ reads NAME, and one rule link one directory too shallow. Corrected in place, since a fold moves bytes and a link written for the old depth resolves nowhere from the new one.
obs THE PLANT FLOOR WAS A PEER'S TO FIX AND THEIRS IS BETTER. I raised the literal from 13 to 14; upstream stopped spelling the number at all, reading the floor from the scan so a lane that adopts passes free and only a control that LOST its import refuses. Theirs took the rebase and mine was withdrawn -- the second time today a seat here built a wall a peer had already published for the same red.
obs THE SECOND PULL RENUMBERED THE ROW A FIFTH TIME AND WITHDREW A FOLD. Upstream had spent %586 while this send was in flight, so the row is %588; and a peer had already folded %578 to REDS-a-grep-for-a-path-rows-578.md, so my duplicate shelf for the same row was deleted rather than published beside theirs -- the second withdrawal in this tree today for a duplicate somebody else landed first.
obs A REPOINTING SED REACHED A PEER'S LOG AND WAS ONLY SAFE BY LUCK. Repointing the withdrawn shelf name I wrote `sed -i ... session-logs/date/20260907/*.kyri`, which is every hand's logs for the day rather than my own two. It changed nothing outside them because the peer's log already spelled the surviving name -- the blind-sweep fault .claude/rules/derived-spine.md names, missed by a glob rather than by a pattern. Name the paths.
